config FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ROBSTRIDE
	bool
	default y
	depends on DT_HAS_FIBRIL_FCAN_ROBSTRIDE_ENABLED
	select MOTOR
	select MOTOR_ROBSTRIDE
	help
	  Compiled whenever the devicetree describes the actuators, which is
	  also what puts RobstrideMotor instances into the schema. Describing
	  the wiring is the whole of adding this function to an image.

	  The driver is selected rather than depended on, because the motor
	  class is off unless something asks for it, and the `motors` phandles
	  have nothing to resolve to without it. A node that names actuators no
	  robstride,bus owns trips the unmet dependency here rather than
	  failing later with an undefined device.

config FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ROBSTRIDE_MAX
	int "RobstrideMotor instances to allocate for"
	default 8
	range 1 32
	depends on FIBRIL_CAN_NODE_ROBSTRIDE
	help
	  The schema's max_count for RobstrideMotor: how many instances the
	  generated code reserves state for. It is a ceiling, not a count. It
	  never reaches the schema blob, so the bus and the ROS graph see only
	  the actuators the devicetree actually names.

	  8 matches MOTOR_ROBSTRIDE_MAX_MOTORS, the driver's own table size, so
	  a board that fits under one does not trip over the other. Lowering it
	  below the devicetree's length fails the BUILD_ASSERT in robstride.c.
